{
  "policy_id": "ld-66-return-to-erasmus-plus-programme",
  "party": "Liberal Democrat",
  "policy_title": "Return to Erasmus Plus Programme",
  "outcome": "O12",
  "verdict": {
    "outcome": "O12",
    "direction": "worsens",
    "magnitude": "minor",
    "time_horizon": "this-parliament",
    "confidence": "moderate",
    "plain": "Rejoining Erasmus+ costs roughly \u00a3570 million per year \u2014 about five times the Turing Scheme it replaces \u2014 adding a significant new spending commitment with no guaranteed offsetting revenue. Economic benefit estimates exist but come from the universities lobby, so cannot carry full weight.",
    "biggest_unknown": "Whether the economic returns from increased student and professional mobility (including inward EU participants and skills gains) are large enough to offset the ~\u00a3460 million net cost increase over the Turing Scheme.",
    "claims": [
      {
        "tier": "stated",
        "claim": "The policy commits to returning to the Erasmus Plus programme as an associated country.",
        "evidence_id": "M",
        "quote": "returning to the Erasmus Plus programme as an associated country"
      },
      {
        "tier": "measurable",
        "claim": "The UK's annual financial contribution for Erasmus+ association in 2027 is approximately \u00a3570 million, already discounted by 30%.",
        "evidence_id": "E20",
        "quote": "The UK's financial contribution for its association with Erasmus+ in 2027 is approximately \u00a3570 million, which includes a negotiated 30% discount on the default contribution rate"
      },
      {
        "tier": "measurable",
        "claim": "The Turing Scheme \u2014 the domestic replacement \u2014 had an annual budget of around \u00a3100\u2013\u00a3110 million.",
        "evidence_id": "E25",
        "quote": "The Turing Scheme typically had an annual budget of around \u00a3100-\u00a3110 million"
      },
      {
        "tier": "projected",
        "claim": "Universities UK International estimates the UK gained a net \u00a3243 million economic boost from Erasmus+ in 2020 after deducting costs \u2014 but this comes from an advocacy/sector body, not an independent fiscal authority.",
        "evidence_id": "E15",
        "quote": "UUKi estimates that in 2020, the UK gained a net \u00a3243 million boost to its economy from Erasmus+ after deducting costs"
      },
      {
        "tier": "projected",
        "claim": "The government expects 100,000 people to benefit in the first year, suggesting scale of outward return on the investment.",
        "evidence_id": "E21",
        "quote": "The UK government expects 100,000 people to benefit in the first year alone"
      },
      {
        "tier": "measurable",
        "claim": "The government has committed to reviewing data from the first year only, leaving the longer-term spending path uncertain.",
        "evidence_id": "E33",
        "quote": "The UK government has stated it will review data from the first year of association (2027/28) to inform decisions on continued participation beyond 2027"
      }
    ],
    "rationale": "Rejoining Erasmus+ raises annual public expenditure from roughly \u00a3100\u2013110 million (Turing Scheme) to approximately \u00a3570 million \u2014 a net increase of around \u00a3460 million per year. This is a real and committed fiscal cost from 2027/28 onward. Against this, the main economic-return estimate (a net \u00a3243 million boost) comes from Universities UK International, an advocacy body with a direct institutional interest in the outcome. Under the source rules, this cannot be the sole or primary basis for a verdict; no independent OBR or IFS costing of the rejoining appears in the evidence. The IFS findings cited (E7/E17) concern individual labour-market outcomes, not macro fiscal returns or Exchequer-level cost-benefit. The government's own position is to review after one year (E33), signalling the fiscal sustainability of continued membership is unresolved. The investment could plausibly raise future human capital and attract EU students and fees (E10, E11), but no evidence unit grounds this at a scale sufficient to offset the cost increase. On the evidence as provided, the net fiscal effect is a spending increase of roughly \u00a3460 million annually for at least one year, with returns speculative at macro level. This modestly worsens the near-term debt path. The 30% discount and review clause reduce but do not eliminate the concern. Magnitude is minor relative to total public spending, and the time horizon is this parliament (commencing 2027/28).",
